### PHASE 3 — Memory & Context Management

**Duration:** 2–3 Days · **Difficulty:** Medium

OpenClaw has basic memory. You need to extend it for a commercial, multi-week service where context grows large and must stay performant.

#### 3.1 Memory Architecture

|Memory Type      |What It Stores                           |Retention             |
|-----------------|-----------------------------------------|----------------------|
|Working Memory   |Current task context, active conversation|Session only          |
|Episodic Memory  |Past tasks, what was done and when       |90 days, then archived|
|Semantic Memory  |Client preferences, habits, key facts    |Permanent             |
|Procedural Memory|How to do recurring tasks for this client|Permanent             |

#### 3.2 Memory Compression (Critical for Cost)

Raw conversation history grows fast. After 7 days, summarise old memories rather than keeping them verbatim. This dramatically reduces context window costs.

> **Weekly Compression Schedule:** Every Sunday at 3am, run a Claude API call that reads the past week’s episodic memory and produces a 3-sentence summary per day. Replace the raw entries with the summary. This keeps the context window lean and cuts API costs by 40–60% over time.

#### 3.3 Claude Code Prompt — Phase 3

```
"Extend the memory.ts module with three tables: episodic_memory (task_id, date, summary,
raw_log), semantic_memory (key, value, last_updated), procedural_memory (task_type,
steps_json). Build a memoryManager.ts that: (1) before each task, fetches relevant semantic
and recent episodic memories and injects them into the Claude system prompt, (2) after each
task, writes a summary to episodic_memory, (3) runs a weekly cron job using node-cron that
compresses episodic memories older than 7 days into single-sentence summaries using the
Claude API."
```

